            <ArticleTitle>Laparoscopic resection and intracorporeal anastomosis of perforated small bowel caused by fish bone ingestion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>572-574</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5505/tjtes.2016.88137</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Presently described is case of a 52-year-old man who was admitted to the emergency department with 3-day history of epigastric pain. Abdominal examination revealed diffuse tenderness and muscle guarding. Plain abdominal X-ray showed free subdiaphragmatic air. The patient underwent diagnostic laparoscopy with presumptive diagnosis of peptic ulcer perforation. Laparoscopy showed several inflamed, edematous jejunal loops with proximal obstruction and perforation by an impacted fish bone. Completely intracorporeal resection and anastomosis using laparoscopic linear stapler was performed and segment of resected bowel was removed through trocar site. Postoperative period was uneventful, and the patient was discharged on fourth day.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
